// File name: projects/12/Array.jack
/**
 * Represents an array.
 * In the Jack language, arrays are instances of the Array class.
 * Once declared, the array entries can be accessed using the usual
 * syntax arr[i]. Each array entry can hold a primitive data type as 
 * well as any object type. Different array entries can have different 
 * data types.
 *
 * There is nothing to this class, and that is the point. A Jack array is not
 * an object with a header and a length -- it is simply a block of consecutive
 * words, and `arr[i]` compiles to an address computation rather than to a
 * method call. So Array.new is a thin alias for Memory.alloc, and the whole
 * class is two lines of real work.
 *
 * Note that `new` is a function, not a constructor: it returns the address
 * that Memory.alloc chose, rather than allocating an object of its own.
 */
class Array {

    /** Constructs a new Array of the given size. */
    function Array new(int size) {
        if (size < 1) {
            do Sys.error(2);    // Array size must be positive
        }
        return Memory.alloc(size);
    }

    /** Disposes this array. */
    method void dispose() {
        do Memory.deAlloc(this);
        return;
    }
}
